-- title:
--   Theorem 10, p. 16 — DIGing converges R-linearly at the explicit rate λ for every step size 0 < α < 1.5(1−δ)²/(μ̄J₁)
-- statement:
--   Consider problem (1), $\min_{x\in\mathbb R^p}f(x)=\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^nf_i(x)$, where the $f_i$ satisfy Assumption 4 (each $\nabla f_i$ is $L_i$-Lipschitz) and Assumption 5 ($\mu_i$-strong convexity with $\mu_i\ge0$, some $\mu_i>0$), and let $x^*$ be its optimal solution. Let the time-varying undirected graphs $E(k)$ and mixing matrices $W(k)$ satisfy Assumption 1 with integer $B\ge1$, and let
--   $$\delta=\sup_{k\ge B-1}\sigma_{\max}\Big\{W_B(k)-\tfrac1n\mathbf 1\mathbf 1^\top\Big\},\qquad J_1=3\bar\kappa B^2\big(1+4\sqrt n\sqrt{\bar\kappa}\big).$$
--   Let $0<\alpha<1.5(1-\delta)^2/(\bar\mu J_1)$ and let $\mathbf x(k)$ be generated by DIGing (Algorithm 1) with step size $\alpha$. Define
--   $$\lambda=\begin{cases}\sqrt[2B]{1-\dfrac{\alpha\bar\mu}{1.5}}, & \alpha\le\dfrac{1.5\big(\sqrt{J_1^2+(1-\delta^2)J_1}-\delta J_1\big)^2}{\bar\mu J_1(J_1+1)^2},\\[2ex] \sqrt[B]{\sqrt{\dfrac{\alpha\bar\mu J_1}{1.5}}+\delta}, & \text{otherwise.}\end{cases}$$
--   Then $0<\lambda<1$, and $\mathbf x(k)$ converges to $\mathbf x^*=\mathbf 1(x^*)^\top$ at the global R-linear rate $O(\lambda^k)$: there is a constant $C>0$ with
--   $$\|\mathbf x(k)-\mathbf 1(x^*)^\top\|_F\le C\lambda^k\qquad\text{for all }k=0,1,\dots$$
--
--   This is the paper's main result for undirected networks: with a fixed step size, gradient tracking over time-varying graphs converges geometrically to the exact optimum, with a rate that is explicit in $B$, $n$, $\delta$, $\bar\mu$ and $\bar\kappa$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The page prints the step-size interval closed at $1.5(1-\delta)^2/(\bar\mu J_1)$; at that endpoint the second formula gives $\lambda=1$, which is no geometric rate, and the proof needs $\lambda^B<1$ ((39), p. 17). The endpoint is therefore excluded. "Doubly stochastic" in Assumption 1 includes nonnegative entries (Setting file). $\delta$ is given as the least upper bound of $\{\delta(k):k\ge B-1\}$. Optimality of $x^*$ is a hypothesis; its existence and uniqueness is a separate item. Lean's $\sqrt[m]{\cdot}$ is `Real.rpow` with exponent $1/m$, applied to a positive base.
-- source:
--   Nedić, Olshevsky & Shi, arXiv:1607.03218v3, Theorem 10, p. 16 (R-linear: footnote 1, p. 1)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_DIGing_Undir_Common
import Definitions.Def_DIGing_Undir_Setting

namespace DIGing.Undir

theorem theorem_10 {n p : ℕ} (f : Fin n → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin p) → ℝ) (Lc mu : Fin n → ℝ)
    (h4 : Assumption4 f Lc) (h5 : Assumption5 f mu)
    (xstar : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin p)) (hopt : ∀ z, objective f xstar ≤ objective f z)
    (E : ℕ → SimpleGraph (Fin n)) (W : ℕ → Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ) (B : ℕ)
    (h1 : Assumption1 E W B)
    (δ : ℝ) (hδ : IsLUB {d | ∃ k, B - 1 ≤ k ∧ d = deltaK W B k} δ)
    (α : ℝ) (hα : 0 < α) (hαmax : α < alphaMax (J1 Lc mu B) δ (mubar mu))
    (x y : ℕ → Stack n p) (hrun : IsDIGingRun f W α x y) :
    0 < rateLam (J1 Lc mu B) δ (mubar mu) α B ∧ rateLam (J1 Lc mu B) δ (mubar mu) α B < 1 ∧
      ∃ C : ℝ, 0 < C ∧ ∀ k : ℕ,
        frob (x k - ones xstar) ≤ C * rateLam (J1 Lc mu B) δ (mubar mu) α B ^ k := by sorry

end DIGing.Undir
